      "text": "분석한 관련 코드야 \nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport re\nimport seaborn as sns\nimport nltk\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nquestions_list \u003d [\n    \"Have you ever made a mistake that ended up saving your life?\", # Q1 (답변 0-9)\n    \"What are some ways to overcome shyness and become more sociable for someone who is naturally quiet, soft-spoken, and reserved, and may also have social anxiety?\", # Q2 (답변 10-19)\n    \"What\u0027s inside a black hole?\", # Q3 (답변 20-29)\n    \"What is the biggest lesson that life has taught you?\", # Q4 (답변 30-39)\n    \"What is your opinion on online dating?\", # Q5 (답변 40-49)\n    \"What quality in others makes you want to slap them?\", # Q6 (답변 50-59)\n    \"What was the most satisfying display of instant karma you have ever seen?\", # Q7 (답변 60-69)\n    \"What\u0027s the nicest thing anyone has ever done for you?\", # Q8 (답변 70-79)\n    \"When someone asks you how you\u0027re doing and you\u0027re feeling awful, what should you say?\", # Q9 (답변 80-89)\n    \"Would you make your company aware if you saw that they accidentally added an extra two zeros onto one of your weekly paychecks, or keep it a secret?\" # Q10 (답변 90-99)\n]\nexample_answers \u003d [\n    \"When I was in my early twenties, my friends and I used to go out almost every weekend to either some party or a nightclub. Often, these locations weren’t in the most safe areas. Being young and feeling indestructible, we kind of liked the excitement of it. Normally, we would meet at a friend’s house, then all pile into one guy’s car. There was always the argument over who would sit in the middle position in the back seat. That was because there was a hump there. I usually eventually volunteered to sit there just to get us moving. One weekend, I got the scheduling mixed up on when I was supposed to meet them. They were ready to go before I got to the friends house but agreed they would wait for me. I told them not to bother waiting and I’d either drive myself or stay home that time. I eventually opted to stay home. The next day I get an excited call from one of my friends telling me how someone had shot into their car for no apparent reason. They were very lucky because although the car was full, the bullet had missed everyone, hitting the middle section of the back seat. The section where I’m 95% sure I would’ve volunteered to sit. I don’t know if the gunshot would’ve killed me, but I know I’m glad I wasn’t there to find out.\",\n    \"Yeah, for some reason I terribly overslept and was three hours late to work. I felt quite bad about the whole thing. This was before mobile emails were a thing and I called in saying that I would be late. So, I walked into the office around 12 PM hoping to avoid colleagues seeing me that late. The first people I saw were the CEO and a bunch of other senior guys, all gathered around my cubicle. “Ok, this is my last day in this job,” I thought. However, the CEO looked at me, relieved, and said “Good morning. Good to see you were late.” Quite an unusual greeting. Only then did I realize what had happened. The hinges holding a huge office air conditioner above my cubicle had failed, and it had gone down right into my seat. The monitor was smashed in two parts, there was a huge bend in the tower case, and the indestructible Cherry keyboard had a few keys missing and a dent in the middle of the metal pad. The air conditioner had fallen at around 11 am, and I came to the office at 12 pm. Should I have come in on time that day, I would have never registered on Quora.\",\n    \"\"\"Yes. It was November 2018, I was going back home after my exam. I hadn\u0027t slept all night and I was dreaming about my warm bed. Now my Exam center was quite far from my home, I had to change Two E-Rickshaws, take metro, and finally take one more E-Rickshaw to reach home. Those of us who live in Delhi-NCR are familiar with E-Rickshaws. It is a battery operated Rickshaw which takes upto 5 passengers. These E-rickshaw drivers don\u0027t move till they get all 5 passengers, (annoying, huh?) I was crossing the road, I saw an E-rickshaw almost full with only one seat left, he was shouting \u0027Metro metro\u0027, I had the biggest smile on my face and waved my hand at him and started running. All of a sudden, a biker came out of nowher e and hit his brakes really hard, His tyres squealed, he came to halt few inches from me. I froze. He yelled at me, I was still frozen, somehow managed to say sorry and thanked him for not running over me. I looked over and the E-rickshaw I was desperately running for, was filled, some lady took the last seat, it was already moving. I was murmuring to myself \u0027 why did you have to rush???? Now we have to wait for 15 minutes till this rickshaw gets filled\u0027 as I boarded the empty E-Rick behind it. I closed my eyes and suddenly heard a loud sound. CRASH . An overspeeding DTC bus rear-ended that E-rickshaw. I was horror-struck. Collision somewhat looked liked like this: All the passengers including the driver were critically injured and rushed to the hospital immediately. I was shocked all the way back. Things really do happen for a reason. Running on the road without looking was probably the best mistake I ever made?\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Yes, because I was an alcoholic, and even when I wasn’t drunk the booze was scrambling my thinking. Like an idiot. I used to run my mouth and do stupid things. (Being an alcoholic was common at that company.) Long story short: because of that I got fired from a company where I had worked 14 years. Of course when I got home I really wanted a drink. But I told myself that I wasn’t going to sit around, jobless, drinking and feeling sorry for myself. I quickly got a low paying job just so I could be working, and then go back to drinking. After that though I never felt the need to drink or get drunk, (and when I drank in the old days I always got drunk). Not that long after that I gave it up entirely. (Sober for this century). It was, only after I left that place, that I realized that the whole thing, working there, drinking, working there etc. was what was making me so depressed, angry, and stupid. I’m sure that if I had continued with that it would have killed me. Funny thing is that none of my drinking “Buddies” called me to see how I was doing.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"My girlfriend and I were visiting NYC, staying at her sisters apartment in the financial district. The evening of 9/10/01 we were on the roof talking about what to do the next day. I looked up at the World Trade Center (about a block and a half away) and recall the top disappeared into a low-lying evening cloud. I asked if people could go the top for the view and was told sure, it\u0027s called Window On the World (or something like that). I suggested we go there before a museum (and, of course, a deli) and my gf said that was a good idea. The next morning I overslept or we would been at the top during the 9/11 disaster. She woke me up after the plane hit and when we went up on the roof of the apt building we could feel the heat. I thought a private plane had an accident since the tower was so large that the impact seemed not so big to me. I suggested we go there to take pictures and as we approached I was reminded I needed to buy more film so we turned back toward a pharmacy. That also probably saved our lives since the tower collapsed as we were on the way back with film. We were covered in ash as we ran for our lives, but at least we hadn\u0027t gotten to the base of the building like we would have had we not gone back for film. I had a slight cough for six months after that nonetheless. Still gives me chills when I realize that my oversleeping, and my gf letting me, saved our lives.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\" I was driving to town to go shopping and I was going to stop after at the post office, which was down the road from the highway. When I for some unknown reason a voice told me to go to the post office first, I turned around and went to the post office. I went in and mailed the letter, walked out got in my car to go back to the highway too drive to town to go shopping. I backed up and started down the small hill, when my brakes failed. I ended up running straight in the middle of the road with no brakes. Thankfully there is not much traffic on this road. I had a voice that told me to go to the post office first. I did what I thought was my own idea, and turned around without thin...\"\"\" ,\n    \"\"\"I happened to me in elementary school. Back in those days, security was very loose in my country, so anybody could enter school facilities after classes ended, even in a private school. I left my classroom, and a couple of middle aged women came to me and told me they would drive me home, saying my mother had agreed to do so. 8 year old me believed them, I didn’t have the concept of kidnapping in my mind. Suddenly I remembered that I had an after-school class and told the two women I had to take that class and left promptly. Of course we didn’t have cell phones back then, so after my class I waited at another spot in the school (I was starting to feel something was off), no sign of these women and there I see my mother walking calmly, no worries. I ran to her and told her all the story and she was shocked, turns out she never received a call and had no idea what I was talking about. I don’t know what would’ve happened if I didn’t have that class or if I simply would’ve agreed to go with them, but I am really happy to have followed my instinct that day.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"I haven\u0027t personally, but my Mother knows a guy who has. He was supposed to be taking a plane trip overseas with a friend of his, when they were like half an hour or an hour from departure his friend realised that they had been shortchanged $100 from the place they were just at. So his friend demanded they be repaid what they were owed. The cashier declined. His friend started arguing with the cashier, which was weird because this was totally out of character for his friend to get so worked up over \"\"only\"\" $100. His friend argued and argued, so he kept trying to get him to give up or they\u0027ll miss their flight. His friend ignored his pleas to just forget the $100 or they\u0027ll have to rebook and everything. Finally his friend was given the $100… But of course they had missed their flight. They both rocked up to the airport and confirmed they had indeed missed their flight… Which they shockingly found out that their missed plane crashed during the flight killing everyone on board. He then thought back on his friends uncharacteristic argument that $100 and believed it had to have been divine intervention.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"This was a couple years back, when I was in only 6th grade. At the time, I was homeschooled, and riding my bike to pick my younger cousin up from the nearby school. I was around 4′5 at the time, and was too short for the oncoming danger to see me. The street I had to cross were connected, so you could easily turn onto the other one. I was stopping in the U-Turn because I had dropped my phone. As I went to pick it up, a bus came crashing by. All I can think, is that if I had kept going, I would have been like a ran over skunk. Dead.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Yes. A year and a half ago, I was getting into some bad stuff with some bad people. I had been doing drugs, going to parties 24/7, fighting with my parents. All the above. I was a bad kid. (A little bit of a back story, I grew up in a very unprotected and abusive childhood. My father is an alcoholic and drug addict. My mom devorced him when I was 12 and since then I’ve suffered from major trauma, anxiety, and depression. I am 17 now.) Anyways, this comes the major event. I was caught stealing a bottle of alcohol. I had been on probation before, for the same thing - stealing. So this was my second strike. I was put on probation for a misdemeanor and thank god they didn’t count me as an adult which they almost did. 6 months probation. At court they ordered me to see a counselor and/or therapist for my actions. A months later I had my first appointment with the lady who changed my life 50%, the other half went to a boy I met at the homecoming dance after my mom moved me to a different school after my first probation meeting. Tasha, she is an amazing wonderful woman. I went through EMDR with her and I was able to get to the root of my every problem. Devon, the boy I met at homecoming, he helped me to open my third eye and got me to stop doing the drugs. He told me every day and imprinted my brain with every fact about every drug I had put into my body. I became enlightened after I realized what I was doing to myself and had to get out of my old patterns. These two people I would have never met if I hadn’t been caught. I am now very spiritual and haven’t put a drug in my body since then. I’ve never been more happy.\"\"\" ,\n    \"\"\"Accept and embrace your soft spoken and sensitive nature. This is who you are right now and it’s okay. This world is loud, noisy, expects you to be good with small talk and make a good first impressions, have a good social life. We may not fit into this environment just yet and maybe never, but that’s okay. Do you have closer friends who you don’t feel anxious with? Are there times you feel at ease, happy and yourself? :) I believe you would have and this is just to show you that you are capable of being self assured in another world where the environment is different. Personally for me, last year, I took a year off and I started traveling. Being alone helped me realised with greater clarity, who I was. I learned that it’s okay to be me, though I am so different from most people. And that out there in the world, many people accept me as I am. They don’t have preconceived ideas of who I am or expectations of me. This has freed me a little bit from social anxiety. I realised it’s okay if somebody doesn’t like me, there will be somebody else who does! And I myself get to choose who I like and who I want to hang out with. I can leave the company of people who make me feel uncomfortable. This knowledge has empowered me. And made me less socially anxious. I still am that awkward girl in the party, but now I know I can approach people who make me feel at ease and I can leave people who make me uncomfortable. And if I want, I can just be alone or leave the party! It’s all up to me. :)\"\"\" ,\n    \"\"\"Try some relaxation methods, such stress-relieving breathing exercises. Break up difficult circumstances into manageable chunks, and focus on becoming more at ease with each one. Instead of automatically assuming the worst, try to pay attention to what others are saying. How to get over your shyness Begin modestly. Discover your strengths. Quit believing that everyone is watching you. Avoid self-destructive behaviour and social circumstances. Good setbacks. Label it. Recognise your shyness. Be in the company of encouraging individuals. (What\u0027s your personality type?) Take a test with us today.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"I overcame my shyness, which is a fear of other people. Start putting yourself out there. Talk to people. (You don’t need to talk for very long.) Look people in the eye. Smile. Practice with strangers and friends alike. At first, it will feel weird to be looking and talking so much with others, but after a while, you will get used to it. Then you will do it at will and with ease. Occasionally someone may not be very nice to you, but most people are really pretty nice. You will lose your fear and have the ability to interact with anybody which is a necessary human ability to possess. Start today. Make goals, like 1) I will look at and smile at three people today 2) I will look, smile and talk to three people today 3) I will talk to everybody I see today. In a month or two, you won’t be shy anymore and looking at, smiling and talking to others whenever you want will be another skill you have learned.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Why should I feel shy or anxiety about something? Ask this to yourself first. What is the answer you get? Whatever may be the answer, you need to face the world. No one is going to be your substitution in your life. You have to play your role by yourself. Being shy and anxiety will never help you to be active. You say you are not active. The reason is you feel shy and anxiety which will keep you lazy most of the time. So, once you are out of your shyness and anxiety, you will obviously be active. Few ways to get rid of shyness and anxiety are Try to face the world as you are. Take both positive and negative comments which has been put on you. Be proud of the positive comments. Think about the negative comments. If those comments doesn\u0027t bother you. Just leave it. In case it bothers, see why have you got that comment. Think an alternate to change the negative into positive. Shyness and anxiety can be gone through only by facing the situation which gives you these two feelings. Once you face it, you would be active henceforth. Train your mind to be active and bold. Tell yourself that you deserve things like happiness, love and life. Edit:By the way, I myself answered this question to come out of social anxiety. This is my first answer. I had too many questions in mind before writing an answer like what will people think about my answer?(even if we doesn\u0027t know each other).. Will I have any view? If I have any view, will I get an upvote? You know I got one upvote and that matters to me. It brought smile in my face and that\u0027s the reason I am here editing. Face it to come out of it dear friends…\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"To overcome shyness and social anxiety, I think it would be best to first address the issue behind the social anxiety. Most likely it is due to low self-esteem. So work on building your self-esteem and self-confidence. You can start out by doing things like daily affirmations each day. Realize that no one is perfect and everyone has flaws. Accept your flaws, then focus on your good qualities, strengths, talents and abilities. Say positive affirmations to yourself daily until it becomes a ritual. Do things that will build your self-esteem such as volunteer work. Volunteering will not only help others but it can also help you by building your self-esteem and self-worth. You might meet new people and also become more social. Learn a new skill and get good at it. Whatever it takes for you to feel better about yourself, do it. This will help you to overcome the fear of being around others in social situations. Take gradual steps to get better, don’t just throw yourself out there. Start out by doing small things, like taking a walk outdoors and saying hi to a person walking by. Then build yourself up gradually until you feel more content being around people and socializing. You have to keep working at it or else if you avoid social situations, it will only make the anxiety worse.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Effective ways to overcome shyness and social anxiety include practicing mindfulness, learning relaxation techniques, and gradually exposing oneself to social situations. Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) can provide structured strategies to challenge negative thinking patterns. Engaging in hobbies or activities that build confidence can also contribute to overcoming these challenges.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"You can daily practice over the mirror. Regular talk in front of mirror by watching you verbal and non verbal expression (attitude, facial expression etc). If you feel more anxiety take social skill training. you can practice breathing exercise when you feel more anxious in social situation. You can also attend on social skill training or get psychological support from clinical or counseling psychologist to overcome social anxiety. Moreover, keep confidence and tell your self“I am confident” “I am confident”.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Yes,you can overcome shyness,since it’s more like a trait of one’s personality,and you can get rid of it if you practice a lot and get yourself out of your comfort zone. You may still have some of your shyness left,but remember that everyone can be shy from time to time,so there’s nothing wrong with it. But Social Anxiety is a mental illness that needs medical treatment and/or therapy. I don’t know if you can fully overcome it - I haven’t heard about such a case,but It gets better If you get help.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Really the only way to overcome it is to put yourself in social situations. It will probably feel awkward at first but it’s the only way to gain experience in socializing and to get comfortable in it. I used to be so shy around people even going to the lunch line at school terrified me because I’d have to talk to the lunch ladies and the other students in the room. I didn’t overcome this until I started to actually talk. Instead of just sitting and eating I made an effort to talk. It started out little but as the weeks went by I became very comfortable with it and started striking up conversations with people not in my friend group. Now that I got over my shyness it’s so relieving to be able to speak my mind without second guessing myself all the time.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"i think its like a muscle, confidence is something you build and work on, everyone has to step out of their comfort zone in life if ever they want to grow and improve and move forward, take it one step at a time, build that muscle, refuse to let anyone or anything set you back, sooner or later you will be able to over come, you may always suffer some anxiety, most people do to differing degrees, but, bravery isnt not being scared, it is being scared and over coming your fear and stepping up anyway\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Interesting question. Inside black hole, nothing can escape even light. It\u0027s a mystery for physicists for over a century. Renowned cosmologist Stephen Hawking and physicist Carl Sagan developed it\u0027s theory. They published a book named black hole containing the fact that there inside a black hole , a point exist which theoretically infinitely massive of high density. This point is called singularity. It exists in centre of a black hole. In the above diagram it\u0027s clear where various parts of black hole exists. Inside black hole through singularity we may go to or discover other universes, through worm hole(Einstein-Rosen bridge). Though scientifically it has no validation of my last statement. Edit 1: General relativity predicts singularities exist at the centers of black holes, but their physical reality is uncertain due to unresolved theoretical questions and the lack of empirical evidence. Edit 2: to create a worm hole, we may need a black hole. A wormhole is a theoretical passageway that connects two points in space and time. It\u0027s a solution to Einstein\u0027s field equations in general relativity.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"We cannot glimpse what lies inside the event horizon of a black hole because light or material from there can never reach us. Even if we could send an explorer into the black hole, she could never communicate back to us. But curiosity doesn\u0027t dine on facts and observation. We humans think of solutions, may be not be exact neither can they be written off as wrong. There are two possible explanation for what exciting, unknown mystery the devil of black hole holds inside itself. Actually there are many, but these two convince me the most.  While my sceptic brain accepts the first theory of singularity, my heart extends it\u0027s total support to the wormhole. Happy interstellar travel.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"I think the proper question should be “What is inside the event horizon of a black hole?” Anyways, the answer is, basically, physicists don’t know. When you approach the event horizon, it’s the point of no return. The needed speed to escape the event horizon of a black hole is faster than the speed of light, which is impossible for any matter. Inside the event horizon, physics go crazy. The calculations suggest that how the fabric of spacetime inside a black hole depends on it’s history. So, what happens if you fall in a black hole? Simply, you’d die. In a stellar-mass blackhole, the tidal forces will be too strong and too fast for you to survive to the event horizon and you’d be dead even before you notice it. If you fall in a supermassive black hole, passing through the event horizon, you might notice some fun light effects but as you go deeper, the gravity will stretch and squeeze you as if you were dough in a bread machine and you will die. So back to the main question, what exists inside the event horizon of a black hole?.. No one knows. There are multiple theories. Some state it contains other universes. Some state it’s just void. But who knows? They are all theories after all.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"What’s inside a black-hole? We know for sure that a black-hole must contain, within its Schwarzschild envelope, a certain amount of energy. We know this by reason of the gravitational effect a black-hole has on stars and other forms of matter that come under the influence of a black-hole’s gravitational force. But we really don’t know just what form this energy may take and how it may be distributed. There are two theories (among others) that attempt to answer the question about black-hole energy and the nature of physics behind the Schwarzschild “curtain’.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"According to theory, within a black hole there’s something called a singularity. A singularity is what all the matter in a black hole gets crushed into. Some people talk about it as a point of infinite density at the center of the black hole, but that’s probably wrong — true, it’s what classical physics tells us is there, but the singularity is also where classical physics breaks down, so we shouldn’t trust what it says here. In a very specific mathematical case, the singularity in a spinning black hole becomes a ring, not a point. But that mathematical situation won’t exist in reality. Others say that the singularity is actually a whole surface inside the event horizon. We just don’t know. It could be that, in real black holes, singularities don’t even exist.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"We don\u0027t know. As far as we can tell, there is nothing that can stop the contents falling in. But we don\u0027t know what will happen when they reach the centre. So we call it a singularity, which means a place where our laws of physics simply don\u0027t work . Also, space and time seem to get crossed over, so that the singularity is in the infinitely far future.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"No one knows whats inside a black hole . Although there are few theories surrounding this . Most popular being Stephen hawking’s theory where he predicts that all particals which go inside will eventually come out of a black hole in the form of radiation known as Hawking radiation . Other theories suggest that Black holes act as a bridge between multiple universes or different points of our own universe ,so you can go inside a black hole and come out at a different place and time .\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"A black hole is basically a dead star. Thier are some homogeneous gases present inside an black hole. In easy way we can say that every start has its own life span and it has a end even though sun is also a start and it will also end one day . So whenever a star dies it geta converted into a black but it a start has less diameter or it is less in size so it will be joint in a black hole which is already present in space. Thus a black is dead star in space which attracts small particles which comes closed to it. picture credit :- unknown\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Inside the black hole there is everything but nothing . all the mass which goes in a black hole goes to a 1 dimentional point called singularity. Singularity is very very small in volume but all the mass of black hole is concentrated at singularity. There can be another universe in a black hole. There is another theory of mixing black holes with warm holes . that is the every object which goes in black hole is thrown out by a warm hole connected to black hole.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Inside a black hole, matter collapses into an infinitely dense point called a singularity, where gravity is extremely strong and space-time curvature is infinite. Surrounding it is the event horizon, beyond which nothing can escape, not even light. General relativity predicts that the very center of a black hole contains a point where matter is crushed to infinite density. It’s the final destination for anything falling into the event horizon.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"TheThe most important lesson Life taught me is “Karma will pay you back”. I am a girl belongs to a middle class family. When I was of 5 yrs, my father had done sucide due to some family problems . I have one brother and one sister. After my father\u0027s death my mother started working in cloth factory for livings . At that time my mother was just 26 .My other family members had raised concern how she can work between mens . They taunted her that she is not caring about family\u0027s reputation. My mother is a strong women. She didn\u0027t even care.She worked.She given us good education. When my sister was of 20 years, my relatives started yelling again , why are you not marrying them.live within your capacity. Again my mother didn\u0027t said anything. She has fullfilled all our needs , given us good manners, good education. At the present , I am working in a IT company. Currently I am in Japan for a project. My brother has started his own business and doing good. My sister is happily married :). Now My relatives have nothing to say. I am 26 now. And now they don\u0027t have anything to say about my marriage. My family members also stopped taunting. And my mother , inspiration of my life, Still likes to work .\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"I worked for a company for 16 years. I was an excellent employee who often went above and beyond. Even the years where the company wasn’t doing great and there were no raises or bonuses, I always gave it everything and then some. The owner was getting old and sold the company he had started and run for 30 years. the new owners were bad, bad people. One Thursday in March 2020 at 5pm, they called a company meeting. They said the company was bankrupt. We were told we could all come pack up our offices the next day. That was it. No going away party, no gold watch, no bonus checks. Not even a thank you. The last weeks paycheck- wasn’t going to be getting it. The 30 days of vacation and personal time I worked so hard to save- gone. Never got to use it or get paid for it. I packed up my office that next day and said goodbye to my longtime coworkers and friends. I decided I would clear my head over the weekend and star a new job search Monday morning. That Monday- was a strange day…there was this thing that came out of nowhere. It was called Covid. I learned not to ever prioritize work over family again. Your job will replace you in a heartbeat. work is important, but there are so many other things that are more important.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"There\u0027s a maid in our hostel. Everyday I greet her with a smile. Even this morning, I said, “ Good morning Aunty, how are you?” She looked at me but did not reply anything. I got angry. But as I was about to leave the mess, I asked her, “Aunty… Is everything alright? You look sad today.” She sat in a chair and I sat beside her. There the story started. She said, “ My husband is admitted in hospital for the last three days and I am unable to take leave from my work as my payment will be deducted and I am in need of money right now. In 2010, he fought against paralysis and it took 1.5 lakhs rupees for his treatment. Now again he has piles. He underwent an operation for this last month but the blood is coming out from the stool again. The doctors are unable to find out the main reason for this and they can\u0027t do anything until the bleeding stops.” She shared all her problems with me for almost half an hour and I was listening to it silently. Hearing all these my eyes filled with tears. Deep inside, I was feeling guilty as I could not do anything for her. I promised myself that one day I will make myself so capable that I could help at least one person in need. I only said everything will be alright aunty. Keep faith in God!” Then, she smiled as she spoke her heart out to me. She was relaxed now and said, “ Thank you for listening beta. I am feeling good now.” So, this incident made me realize that sometimes all you can do for someone is lending your ears attentively. It does help a lot. Everyone you meet is fighting a battle. You never know what pain they are going through. Be kind always!\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"One morning, I woke up very motivated, called a friend and said “ Let’s go to the park to exercise!” “ That’s great news, Hector. You know I’m there every morning, I’ll see you soon!” Peter said. I don’t know what was going on that day, but I felt like I could have run a full marathon, so I challenged my friend. “ Hey, Pete, I feel good. I bet I can beat you running this morning. ” Luckily he ignored me. I was a heavy smoker and found myself in terrible physical condition. What was I thinking? Somehow this sudden spark of motivation made me think that giving my 100% that morning would compensate all my bad habits in a single day. I ran and ran… and ran until my heart rate was going through the roof. Then… reality kicked in! I felt lightheaded, lost my vision, and I fell to the ground. I felt like the earth was moving in circles. Pete was there trying to help me. “Should I call an ambulance? What should I do?” He asked nervously. What was I thinking? It was just a spark of motivation. The next day, I felt like a car had run over me. I gave up. To answer your question, what important lesson has life taught you? I’ve learned that there are no shortcuts in life. Today I frequently challenge my kids asking them to draw the path where their daily actions will take them. More social media? Draw the path. More exercise? Draw the path. More reading? Draw the path. What are you reading? Draw the path. Who are your friends? Draw the path. More partying? Draw the path. You get the idea. The bottom line is this: It’s not rocket science. Our destiny is surprisingly related to our DAILY HABITS, not sparks of motivation.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Keep in touch with your friends. July 2017 , I was one the rare people from IIT who didn\u0027t have a job. My start up which was just of paper had died a year ago. Then another opportunity of being a Co-founder came, and I had to let it go due to some disagreements. So I\u0027m sitting in staying in a hostel without permission( thanks to my friends). I have no job. No place to live. Not much money. And I had no idea what to do! I decided to sit for placements again. They give you another chance if you want it. And even though it was one of the most horrible experiences of my life. I went for it. Engineering wasn\u0027t my game. I knew it. So I started to look towards management profiles. Meanwhile, I also started an online internship at IIM Lucknow. 4th August , Then came the friendship day. I missed a lot of them since we all graduated a month ago. So as I was jobless I started sending “HAPPY FRIENDSHIP DAY” to most of my friends. Got a lot of great responses. Connected with a few old friends as well. One of my close friends asked what I was doing and I couldn\u0027t brush him. I revealed I was jobless as fuck. He mentioned there is a startup in our IITM Research Park, they need someone creative. I wasn\u0027t quite interested as I made up my mind for the placements. But he mentioned they are doing great work, just go and meet them. One of my friend was already working there. He told me about the company. It sounded nice. Then another friend( a class topper) joined the company. I trusted his judgement. But still just went in to meet casually. They wanted a game artist. I had zero experience. But I did do art as a kid. After 4 interviews and one assignment. I got the job. It\u0027s been a year now. I\u0027m now at the position of content manager. And I absolutely fucking love my job!\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"One of the most important lessons that I have learned in life is to \"\"face your fears.\"\" Fears can hold us back from experiencing everything that life has to offer. When we give into fear we limit ourselves. We think we can do only this—go only that far—but no more. My fear was a fear of failure. I was so afraid that I would fail that I failed to take action. This was a self-fulfilling cycle that kept me from achieving goals that I had in my business and personal life. I was so paralyzed by fear that I did nothing. I would get excited to do something, and get ready to do it—and then the fear would take over and I would quit. 2 . Lesson Two - Treat Other People As You Want To Be Treated The second most important life lesson that I learned is to treat people like you want to be treated. This is the \"\"Golden Rule.\"\" It is a Biblical principle to \"\"Love your neighbor as yourself.\"\" I wish I could asy that I have always followed this principle but I haven\u0027t. I was a \"\"me\"\" person - What\u0027s in it for me? What can you do for me? I didn\u0027t mean to be, I just was. I\u0027m not sure when I changed but I know that I did. I no longer get impatient with people. I help where I can. I smile at people. I like people so much more now and you know what? They seem to like me more to. 3. Lesson 3 - Take chances You have no chance of succeeding, no chance of reaching your goals and being all you can be unless you take a chance and take action.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"The Biggest Lesson in Life is NOT FAILING ENOUGH! My mother and father grew up poor. When they started working they worked hard, they never got much education or overseas travel. They were the second generations of immigrants when the communist China when through hard times in the early 1920s. So when my parents had me, I was given everything. Good educations, exposure to luxury and travels. Because my parents failed alot in their lives, they are worried their kids would fails. So i was well sheltered and always discipline to understand risk and steer away if any element of risk ahead. I left home when I was 19 to the UK for studies. I’ve always prepared before I embarked into anything from exams, interviews, work or even conflicts. I never took risk, I never walk on the fine line between danger and safety. I was educated not to fail. But the problem with not failing is not learning things the hard way. Up till I wad 30, I never had experience of real life. I was playing too safe and failing was not an option. Looking back, i have missed a lot of opportunities and life experiences. Yesterday, i was at my parents-teacher meeting for my 6 years old. The teacher said that my daughter was too shy to speak up in school because she was worried she might get the answer wrong. I just felt disappointed that the cycle is repeating again. There is no right or wrong answer in life. You have to make your own decision. We need to fail in life before we can see the success unfold before your eyes.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"No body gives a damn unless you are successful or rich or beautiful or all of them. You are replaceable. Everywhere. Life isn\u0027t fair. Its screws you royally. Learn to deal with it. Bestfriend, boyfriend, girlfriend- all of them will leave. Just wait. At the end what matters is result. “ Safar khoobsurat h mazil se bi” This is a lie. Even your parents will doubt you and your potential if you won\u0027t be able crack entrance examination. No matter how hard you love, if its not meant to be, it won\u0027t happen. People who are closet to you, will betray you. Karma is real.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Your life is totally your responsibility . I will repeat again - your life is totally your responsibility . Please stop keep blaming others if they distract you . Its only mean you dont have any self control . If you dont have money and you want little more keep oiling to the person for whole life . This one is something from my personal experience -Looks are not everything .yes, Looks have advantage . Looks have advantage to the dumb and imatuare person only . TRUST ME I can give you so many example . Its just an excuse that she looks better so she is getting upvote . with the time everything gets complicated so do it now .\"\"\" ,\n    \"\"\"No matter how wrong people are, they always justify their actions to convince everyone they are right instead of admitting the mistake. I have always put this my priority. And, I made this promise to myself. Whenever I make a mistake , I will be the one who acknowledges it. If someone finds out and asks me about it, I will not spend the first five minutes arguing about how right I am. Instead, I admit my own mistake. Even if I have a reason for what I did, it comes afterward. I expected everyone to be like this. With time, as I get accustomed with the world, I realise not everyone possesses this trait. Everybody is busy proving themselves right. Even though you are in agony due to their actions, they will act like they had no choice, before an apology just for the sake of it. I had one such incident even yesterday. So one big lesson I have learned is. Never expect anything in return. Being good to someone doesn\u0027t ensure they reciprocating the same.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"It’s the only kind of dating I’ll probably ever do. Bear with me for a second. We live in a society where men are expected to make the first move, almost always. But it’s exceedingly easy to be labeled a creep when your advances are unwanted. And the idea that I might be bothering someone who doesn’t want to be bothered mortifies me. Everyone on a dating site is basically saying: “Hey, I’m available! Talk to me.” And if a woman is not interested in me, I at least know that I wasn’t bothering her while she was just trying to enjoy a night out with her friend. There are some downsides, of course. Overall, though, I still find it preferable to the alternative.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Online Dating is like a Mirage in a Desert. From a distance, it looks like water but as you get closer, it is that scorching hot sun and you are going to die thirsty. I am from pre-online dating era and I was in a steady relationship for over a decade before we took a definite break for a year. This is when I tried online dating, as a distraction from my break situation and to give space to my partner. As a self growth addict, I tend to have this habit of making every scene in life a chance to learn something. So my focal point was to double this app as a tool where I would meet people, learn and improve myself, as weird as this sounds. If possible, I wanted to experience and tackle rejections. I have always had a curiosity to make myself immune to rejections although I have hardly been through them because I am a content introvert who does not seek out things that I may not really do well in. Now I am a moderately attractive female of 33, Indian descent and lived outside India. So I began without any expectations, as in my mind, I am a much older female. I have only been in one relationship prior to that and so was relatively naive in comparison to people my age. But here is what my personal experience has been. By the way, the break came to an end. He wanted me back. He spoke to me in clear and calm words. I could see through his intentions. It was pure. I am back to being monoamorous. Yayyy.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"At the moment I’d say my stance is pretty clear. If I said I didn’t like it I wouldn’t be one to talk. But I have no problems with it, so online dating in my opinion is okay. Of course, it does come with flaws. At the moment there aren’t any Quoran couples that are well known, but I can think of past incidents with Yui Lee and Claire Burns, as well as Nathan and Emmie. Oh and Alex Wei and her sad excuse for flirty friend material. Online dating and relationships do turn out good sometimes, they’re just harder to make work than a college dropout on his 6th can of Mountain Dew Code Red. I think online dating is fine, but I’m definitely sure anybody would prefer close proximity relationships rather than online ones.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"It’s one of the things that I will never do, I don’t know if I will be able to answer this question accurately, but there is only one thing that I know: there is no spark, not that spark during chats when you feel when you see them in real. I see so many people who have in their bio’s like “I like someone who is special to my heart but I’m down to date anyone who likes me” Bruhh— you like someone? And also you want to date others? Do you even respect that person? Or at least your feelings? The other day one of my close friends told me about this girl, who became his friend recently, then they became “kinda close” and then they started to date. Before revealing to me that he was dating her, he told me that he will never date a girl like her who had so many boyfriends and crushes. Typical? I know. But then he started dating her and he told me that “not all relationships work”, “some are just meant for timepass”. Of course, yeah, the majority of the people who date online do it just for timepass. Right now I am at an age where dating is like: ARE WE DOIN\u0027 THIS SHIT OR NOT? CAUSE I HAVE TONS OF OTHER WORKS TO DO. If both of them are honest with their feelings then it\u0027s totally fine but if it isn\u0027t like that then get your ass out, search a date and be in a relationship if you are dying to be in one. \"\"Somewhere, someone is searching you in everyone they meet cause they know their choice is special.\"\"\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"The structure of my life does not leave a lot of room for me to meet someone new. I am busy, have established groups of friends and go about a routine where I encounter the same people. Online dating places at my fingertips hundreds of potential dates that have already been somewhat sorted thanks to the information I have entered regarding my preferences. If I meet a stranger somewhere I know nothing about him. Online I can look at someone\u0027s photos, his profile and how he has answered basic questions. Even if he is not stating the truth I am able to glean a lot from how he writes, how he expresses himself and what he chooses to say. For me, online dating is practical and - with a few precautions - safer than meeting someone I have no information about. I am enthusiastic about online dating and it\u0027s in fact how I met the man currently known as Boyfriend. My adventures online dating: Dushka Zapata\u0027s answer to How do women over 40 recover from divorce? What\u0027s it like to start over and how do you begin?\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"People should be patient. There is no doubt that even the most popular dating app or site will have fake accounts, let alone others. Online dating can be successful and joyful if you find a right one, but it may takes you many years to find the one. If u just use them for a few days and give them up later, then I think there is no app for u, because you do not have patience on finding sb. Even if IRL, it will take a long time to find a right people, let alone online which is a more virtual world. Many people always find their love, but they do not know what they’re really looking for. Let’s wait for a while. Do not be hurry. Love happens when you’re not looking for it. The min someone stops chasing it, love will present itself out of nowhere. Any age at anytime.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"For me, online dating is just a choice. For example, when I feel bored and lonely, I may log in and check if any nice person who can make me feel happy. For me, it is just a choice. If I want, I may oepn those famous dating sites. If I am tired of online chatting , I may delete them and have a rest. As a trans girl, I need to admit that online dating sites are great choice for me. In real world, not all people can understand my choice.Actually, some people leave me after I choose to be a trans girl. Of course, I don’t regret to make this decision. Chatting with some friendly people on those trans dating sites is not a bad decison. Of course, I don’t force myself to use those sites. If I am tired of chatting online, I will have a rest.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"If you are good at reading people and filtering out scammers after a few interactions, it’s a decent way of quickly meeting lots of potential matches.. You need to have thick skin and a solid sense of self-worth going into it.. If you are insecure and fall for instant relationships and the grand promises they come with before carefully evaluating people.. the internet will eat you alive.. I don’t know of anyone who hasn’t had unpleasant experiences while dating online.. that said, the ones who were successful got good at reading between the lines of the profiles.. they didn’t take the negative experiences personally.. they also didn’t just pick partners based on the photos..\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Online dating can be a fantastic way to meet new people and potentially find a meaningful connection. it opens up a world of possibilities, allowing you to connect with individuals you might not have crossed paths with in your everyday life. the key is to approach online dating with an open mind and realistic expectations. remember, not every interaction will lead to a lasting relationship, and that\u0027s okay. be honest in your profile, stay safe by not sharing personal information too quickly, and trust your instincts when deciding whether to meet someone in person. take your time getting to know the person through messaging and video calls before arranging a face-to-face meeting. ultimately, online dating can be a fun and rewarding experience if you approach it with a positive attitude and a willingness to be yourself. you do you and enjoy the journey! I reported my romance scammer to “specter “lynx “cóm and they helped me recover my scammed money.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Online dating, to some, is the greatest thing since sliced bread and the way that they met their partner. However, for most, it is an exercise in futility and frustration. If you are pretty good looking, then you will probably be successful. If not, then welcome to frustration world. Use the money that you would spend on a dating site and use it to pursue a hobby or a passion. You’re more likely to spend your time with a person with like mind. And who knows what happens from there. :)\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"One of my closest friends, we\u0027ll call him Luke, was dating a girl, we\u0027ll call her Satan ica. Luke was absolutely whipped by Satanica. She was beautiful, too beautiful. And he was Satanica\u0027s slave. He drove her to class in college because she didn\u0027t feel like driving her own car. He scheduled her hair appointments. Anytime she had any sort of request he immediately obliged. Satanica gladly and thanklessly burned through every dollar Luke owned. On more than one occasion I saw them in a heated disagreement where Luke was absolutely in the right but he just caved and said sorry, tucking his tail between his legs. The worst part? Satanica cheated on him twice and he immediately took her back both times. I loved Luke. He was my closest friend. But I wanted to slap the living hell out of him.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Well, I feel like slapping those who indulge in shoulder surfing especially in offices. ​ It is so annoying to find someone standing right behind me, peeping into my computer screen or mobile phone while I am texting. Dude, it could be some personal conversation. Don’t you have anything better to do? I was actually so pissed off with this habit of colleagues that once I stuck a sticky note on my computer monitor which stated this: If someone looks at your computer screen or mobile phone while you are typing or texting, just open Google and start typing - how to kill the person who is reading my text. Why on earth do you do that!\"\"\" ,\n    \"\"\"My elder cousins possess a few qualities and I don\u0027t just want to slap them, I want to beat the living daylights out of them . Here are the qualities- No matter what I do, I will not get any compliments. Instead I will get shit load of negativity. I am afraid of being on stage but once in 10th standard I gave a presentation to the class and my principal was really happy with it, so was I. I was telling this to mom and that smug bastard said “Va zyada ud mat, zameen pe aa ja!” Or “You don\u0027t need to be this happy.” Basically he brought down my entire happiness level single handedly. My relatives are born winners here. I am an introvert so I do not talk a lot. But instead of understanding this, one of them said “Ekdam ladki hai tu” meaning “You’re just like a girl”. Apparently because only girls are shy. They still don\u0027t understand the concept of introversion, they think we just need to ‘be a man’ and grow a pair of balls. And they are not even ready to accept the fact that not everyone is same. They think they can never be wrong. They are elders how can they be wrong? Whatever they do is right. They will always prove how intelligent they are. There are 2 ways of doing that - They always choose the latter.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"People\u0027s over-obsession over the idea of feminism. In a recent interview, Kangana Ranaut was quoted saying that there are three heroes in the movie Rangoon i.e.. Shahid Kapoor, Saif Ali Khan and herself. When the media asked Shahid about this, he said:  Why doesn’t she say there are three heroines in the film? Is there anything less in being a heroine? There are three protagonists in the film and for me, being a hero or a heroine doesn’t make any difference. So, if saying that you being a hero makes you a bigger person, I don’t agree with that thought process. And I couldn\u0027t agree more for it is \u0027not\u0027 demeaning to call woman, a woman. Why should anyone call a strong independent decision-making woman the \u0027man of the house\u0027? She can be strong and still be called a woman. \"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"That traffic cop who instantly pulls out my motorcycle keys as if I came there to return his bike. When in traffic jam, people behind you honking unnecessarily. When your BEST buddy remembers you only when he needs some favour. When someone takes a selfie and uploads it on Facebook or Instagram without my permission. When my relatives ask me \"\"what are you doing these days?\"\" while eating chips. That face... irritates me.\"\"\" ,\n    \"\"\"People who make crass jokes on others looks. Such people should definitely be slapped. Pretentious behaviour. This does irritate me. But it\u0027s amusing to watch pretentious people as well. :D People spitting on roads. Instantly teleport next to them and SLAP! People who jump the queue. A slap is just not enough here. People who drive rashly on the roads. Slap! Punch! Kick! and blow them up with a nuclear bomb!\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"Slap? Not just slap, Sometimes I want to stab a knife through Brain. When Someone during a discussion calls you a knowledge-less person or present some logic-less arguments or being a rug rat when they post some shit like \"\"Arguing with idiots is like playing chess with a pigeon; no matter how good you are at chess the pigeon is just going to knock over the pieces, crap on the board and strut around like it\u0027s victorious.\"\" and they pretend like they know everything and consider himself god but actually they sucks.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"Judging someone before you even get to know them . My dad\u0027s brother, my so-called-uncle, went around the whole family spreading rumours about me. I even found out he had termed me a \u0027Slut\u0027. My uncle! I didn\u0027t know about any of it unless I had to move in to one of my other uncle\u0027s house in Bangalore for a few days (unless I found accommodation elsewhere). They were weird in the beginning. They used to randomly check my bag and be suspicious of me. I thought that\u0027s how they were. I was being looked at differently. Didn\u0027t care. Thought it was just a matter of few days. My aunt didn\u0027t like it when I got too close to her son. He\u0027s my brother for crying out loud! Days passed by, they got to know me better. They confessed and apologised for treating me differently. I don\u0027t understand why people treat someone on the basis of some piece of gossip!\"\"\", \n    \"\"\"Sometimes people just happen to disrespect a person as a human being just because he/she is doing a job of low social status than that of theirs.Recently I came across this ​ ​and this.. ​ ​These are just symbolic to what I\u0027m trying to say. In our daily life we often come across people who behave in a disrespecting manner with people such as waiters, delivery boys, cleaning employees, house maids and many such low status jobs. I mean come on, these guys are also people like you and me doing their jobs. Without them OS lives wouldn\u0027t be as comfortable as it is now.  Some folks seem to enjoy pelting stones at animals, beating them for no reason, holding them up by their tails, using them for entertainment and what not. These people seriously need to be slapped .  ​ ​ Some people just can\u0027t say no. Instead they give commitments in the begining and then they give excuses.  ​Dudes like these surely have talent but not common sense. They have right to do whatever they wish with their lives but not with the lives of others who are riding along with them.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"People who comment on every other thing whether they know about the topic or not, even if it doesn\u0027t concern them. Examples: People who comment on every other thing whether they know about the topic or not, even if it doesn\u0027t concern them. Examples: How often do we come across people like this? These are just few examples off the top of my mind. They don\u0027t realize that they may be hurting someone. Most of the times they come off as idiots anyway, so why do it? Nothing grinds my gears more than this.\"\"\" ,\n    \"\"\"My sister and I (we were around 8 or 9) were at the beach building a sandcastle when out of nowhere, a boy around our age, stomped on our sandcastle! We were shocked at this unprovoked attack but were not the sort to tattle. His father, seeing what his son had done, told him not to do it again. The son didn\u0027t even respond. I got the feeling he was in the habit of disregarding his parents. My sister and I moved a short ways away and rebuilt our castle. STOMP! followed by obnoxious laughter from the brat. My sister and I looked at each other then again, started to rebuild. This time we made a revision: we inserted a sharp stick into the tallest tower of our castle. STOMP! then a loud shriek of pain from bratty boy. As he runs crying to his dad, my sister and I watched dolefully, not sure if we were about to get in trouble. “Daddy, those girls put a stick in their castle and hurt my foot!”, the boy wailed like he\u0027d been bitten by a shark. “Well son, I told you not to step on their castle,” replied dad mildly. The boy sniffled a few more times and pouted, but left us alone thereafter. Sometimes you have to make your own karma.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"This is petty as hell so you can beat me up in the comments but here goes. When I was 11 years old, I had a 16 year old cousin who was glamorous. She wore heels and made fun of the fact that I couldn’t. No big deal, right? Her teasing was relentless, though because I could not walk in heels to save my life. One day her mother took us to a wedding and as usual she wore heels. She laughed at how I walked in kitten heels and proceeded to show me how to walk properly. She stood at the top of the stairs, took one step and proceeded to tumble down the entire flight of stairs. The irony is, for over two decades, I trained some of the most accomplished runway models in the world for fashion week in Milan, Paris and NY. My job was literally training women how to walk in heels.\"\"\", \n    \"\"\"I was driving in Yellowstone park many years ago. There was stopped traffic and a bit of snow on the ground. There were many beautiful bison meandering around the stopped traffic. A man got out of his red pickup truck laughing, he made a snowball and hit a huge bison square on the forehead. Still laughing the man jumped back in his truck, and shut the door. The traffic was not moving at all. The bison stared at the closed door of the red truck for about a minute, and then he slowly walked over to the drivers side door. He then slowly head butted the door with his enormous horns, causing severe damage to the truck. You could hear the man screaming profanities through his closed window of the now misshapen truck.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"We were going to China on a trip to visit Beijing, Shanghai, Chengdu and Xian. At the New Delhi airport, there was a group of Chinese tourists who were returning to China after visiting India. They were asked to fill some forms and were struggling to fill it in English. One of them requested me, and i happily obliged. Soon there was a small line of Chinese tourists getting there forms filled by me. I didn\u0027t mind as flight was not leaving for next 2 hours. We reached Beijing and realised that the hotel cab that was supposed to pick us up, was nowhere to be seen. We tried calling and the hotel reception, but they did not had an English speaking staff in the night. For the people who have not visited China, you should know that Hotel has different address and names written in Chinese and English. If you are not carrying Chinese name and address, its very difficult to ask for directions. New to China, we felt stuck and helpless at Airport. Soon we saw the entire group of tourists i had helped. Seeing me and my wife they waved and asked if we need any help. They immediately made a call to the hotel reception and explained the situation in Chinese. They also waited untill our cab from hotel arrived at Airport. I really believe that was one act of kindness that was immediately returned back to me as an instant karma!\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"Many years ago, I had to make the fifteen-mile drive from school to home to fetch something I’d forgotten and couldn’t do without. As I left town on the way home, I noticed that the highway patrol had set up a speed trap for traffic bound into town. On the way back, I got caught on a two-lane highway behind a guy who wanted to do 40 in a 50-zone. Except when I tried to pass him in one of a few passing zones, when he’d speed up so that my VW bug couldn’t get around him. As we approached the final hill that preceded the long flat straightaway coming into town, I backed off a hundred-fifty yards or so and accelerated to about 80. As we went up that hill, I closed in on him at a considerably higher speed than he was going and pulled out, just short of the crest, in a no-passing zone, of course, as if to pass him. Naturally, he could, and did, accelerate very quickly to prevent my passing him—which I had no intention of doing. I jammed on the brakes just in time to be in the lane where I belonged, doing a sedate 45, when I crested the hill. Meanwhile, he’d crested the hill doing at least 80. I waved at him as I passed his car, which the cops had nailed.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\" I used to work as a hotel manager. We had a youth group and their leaders staying there one weekend. As they were checking out, one of the leaders was at the front desk yelling and screaming about how he didn’t sleep all weekend bc of the noise from the kids in the rooms next to theirs. I had put the entire group on the first floor with each other so that the leaders could keep tabs on their kids. So all the rooms surrounding his room was HIS kids. When I pointed that out he got even more upset saying the hotel staff was responsible for making sure the kids were quiet in the rooms after a certain time. I let him know, that no we were not responsible for the behavior of his group. Needless to say he didn’t like that and continued to berate me on his way out the door. He had his back to the door and wasn’t paying attention to it constantly opening and closing because of his proximity to the sensors. He turned to walk out and the doors closed on him, trapping him between the frame and the door. It was all I could do not to giggle in glee and delight when that happened.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"I was taking a night train home from work in Chicago when it was cold and raining heavily that night. I had a monthly train pass as well as per ride pass. In that train there was another foreign passenger. He didn’t speak English nor had a train ticket. The train conductor stopped in our section and realized the foreigner didn’t have a train ticket. As a result, he asked him to get off the next station. I felt bad for the guy because it was raining, cold and he didn’t speak English. I asked the conductor to use my per ride pass for the guy. The conductor asked, “Why did you do that? Do you know the guy?” …I said, “ I didn’t know him but I know it’s not fun to be in such a situation. Who knows, maybe someday I might be in this situation and someone will help me.” Few months later, I took a weekend train to work and forgot my purse at my workplace. The conductor is not the usual person, so the new person didn’t trust that in a frequent rider. He asked I get off the next station. Then suddenly, a man comes and asks the conductor to use his per train pass on me. I thanked him and he said, “No problem. Maybe someday, when I’m in need, someone will help me.” .. those words gave me goosebumps as it was the exact words I conveyed few months ago. In that moment, I knew what you do whether good or bad, comes back to you. The law of karma.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"Some people like to kick the back of the seat in front of them when in a bus or airplane. A very, very nasty habit. Also a pretty safe one, as it seems people rarely turn around and ‘do something about it’. Even though that’s pretty much what you’re doing, isn’t it? You are broadcasting to the world: “I’m a major asshole, do something about it!” Now imagine if you will, riding a bus. It’s one of those nice cozy night buses with curtains on the side of the windows that you can sleep in. You lean back a little and kick the back of the chair in front of you. No big deal, you do it all the time. Did it a million times before, and surely this won’t be any different. That weak little coward in front of you won’t say or do a thing, haha! You’re SUCH an alpha male, Lev! Only this time, the president of your country, happened to ride the bus, incognito with a hoodie on to “experience how the common people ride buses”. Your country is Russia. The President’s name? Vladimir Putin.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"When I was doing an internship over the summer, I had to use public transit very frequently. People are usually decent, albeit a few entitled people. This story is about one of the selected few. So it was 6:50 am, and I was waiting for the bus to arrive. I was already sweating, and the sun barely showed itself yet. People around me were quite anxious on this Monday, call it Monday syndrome, whatever. Basically, nobody was in a great mood. After a couple of minutes under the sun, we saw the bus a couple hundred meters away. At that point, as an unspoken rule, people started forming a line based on how far away from the curb they were currently standing, like they always used to. So I join and we have this line made, and the line gets tighter as the bus comes closer, with people coming in closer from the back to get on quicker. Then comes the entitled woman. I’ll call her Karen. She was in her mid-to-late 20s, dark purple sunglasses on, browsing the phone the whole time we were standing up. The bus stop only had seats for three people, and she was sitting in one when I arrived. Naturally, she was quite a ways back from the start of the line when it was assembled. So, what does she decide to do? She cuts the line like it’s none of her business. Now, in the UK, you might get some rude stares but not here. In Turkey, people tend to speak out about these kind of events, with the hope of publicly shaming the offender. So a man in his 50s turns to her and very calmly says, “If you don’t get back to where you are, you are not getting on this bus.” Karen, who is now enraged at this man’s behavior, starts raising her voice (rough translation): “Hello? I was sitting back there, how dare you take my spot? Move along please (as she tries to squeeze in between the opening by the man and the door) thaaank youu.” The man, lets her pass and have her way, because I imagine he is already frustrated because it’s Monday, and it’s quite hot. Well, she gets on, and that’s when karma strikes: She doesn’t have enough money on her card. The screen flashes red, and after seeing that we enter the bus one by one, beep beep green light, and we sit down. She goes around and asks nearby people for a swipe in exchange for money. She finally finds someone who agrees and she swipes. This time, she is the one red with embarrassment, and to top it all off, all the seats were taken by the time she scanned the card, so she is forced to stand up the entire way. Karma is a female dog sometimes.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"I was driving one day and unfortunately wasn’t paying attention to what’s around the bend on the road. I came up to a pedestrian crossing where the lights had turned red. I instantly jammed the brakes to stop my car as I saw a man already cycling across the road (the lights for him to cross had already turned green). In that moment I did see immense horror all over his face, and I can imagine I made his heart skip a beat as he probably thought I was going to run him over. He was so furious he stopped cycling and took to unleash his fury at me. I totally understood his reaction and did apologise profusely. I kept repeating, and repeating “Im so sorry, please forgive me, I wasn’t paying attention.” It seemed the more I apologised the more profanity and insults he hurled at me. I continued to apologise as I genuinely felt really bad, but he carried on disinterested in my apology, all while blocking my path. I didn’t know what else to do or say. He finally decided to continue along the pedestrian crossing as traffic was building behind me. As he cycled, still using profane language, he kept turning to me and giving me the… one finger salute. That was not a good move on his part. This time, his lack of attention to where he was heading caused him to collide straight into the traffic light ahead. In an instant he was off his bike. He was unhurt, but now even more livid. As I drove off I have to admit I did smile. I believe karma got him for not showing any mercy, and for not being thankful for not getting seriously hurt. The way I see it… If everyone was so unkind and unrelenting people would see no need to apologise, express remorse or reflect on mistakes. That man went too far with his anger, and karma stepped in with lessons for all. That’s why its my favourite instant karma moment.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"This was 3 months back when I was in China for a student exchange program. It had been 2 days and I was in a supermarket searching for basic food stuffs. Everything was in Chinese and hardly anybody understood what I was trying to ask. I was almost on the verge of crying because I realized these 3 months were going to be very tough. Then, a man approached me and tried to ask using hand gestures if everything was okay. I asked him where I could find ketchup. He didn\u0027t know English. But, he realized my dilemma and called his daughter from his cellphone. She talked to me in English and asked me what I needed. She then translated that back to her father and he took me to the section. It was a very touching moment of my life. Even in a strange country, if someone tries to do even this much for you, it is enough in taking a leap of faith.\"\"\" ,\n    \"\"\"This incident happened when I was doing an internship in France. On a weekend I had gone to visit Bordeaux. I had planned to roam round the city and return back on the same day. Unfortunately, I missed the last train back to my place. I didn\u0027t have enough money to get an accommodation. Meanwhile, I met two guys from Pakistan who probably sensed my plight and approached me. On asking I told them about my condition and without hesitating they invited me to their place. I was kinda relieved but a little skeptical. Anyways, without much options left, I decided to accompany them. On reaching at their place I realized that these people were illegal immigrants and several others were staying at this place. Despite of the scarcity they provided me with a separate bed and homemade food. Next day I left with a different attitude towards life and people. I have made a point to be kind and helpful towards others.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"I was in Walt Disney World with my son, Daniel, who is autistic. At the time was 7 years old. My wife and our three other sons were with us too. He was having one of his melt-down screaming tantrums, God only knows why. So my wife and I decided I would take the bus back to the hotel, and she would stay in the park with the other three children. And the screaming continued... We waited for the bus as the screaming continued. Then we got on the filled to capacity bus, and the screaming continued. As the bus wound around the many stops it had to make before arriving at our destination, the screaming continued. My son looks \"\"normal\"\". To the many people on the bus, he appeared to just be a kid screaming - or a spoiled brat who did not get his special toy that day. It did not take long - less than a minute - before the screaming prompted stares, then glares from the other passengers. To my utter shame, I found myself losing my temper at my own son. I was embarrassed, and angry, and frustrated, and felt cheated by God for not being able to enjoy a normal vacation with our family because of this damn affliction of my son. And then I realized I was close to losing it too. I started to wonder what life would be like without having to deal with this cross. And just as the reactions by the other passengers was becoming most intense, a man seated just in front of me turned around to face me. I braced myself for his onslaught of advice on how to raise a well-behaved child. Then he said calmly, \"\"Is he alright?\"\" I said, \"\"He\u0027s autistic.\"\" Then the man said, \"\"It\u0027s alright.\"\" And he smiled. That\u0027s all. And suddenly, all the anger building up inside me was gone. I almost wept for shame at how I had felt a moment before. It no longer mattered what the other people thought. My son was my gift from God again. I will never forget that man, nor his small, simple act of kindness and understanding. I truly believe he was my son\u0027s guardian angel that day, maybe mine too.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"Last year I moved to California to be with a guy I thought I was in love with . Five months later, I spoke with my sister-in-law and told her things weren\u0027t working out and I want to come home. My brother Neil called me the next hour to tell me he can pick me up tonight. (We live in Atlanta) I told him tomorrow will be fine. He was there around 10:00 the next morning. We stayed in a hotel that day and flew back home the following morning. At first I thought, it\u0027s a brotherly thing...nothing special. But after telling my friends and family, and hearing their unexpected responses, I realized I have an exceptional, phenomenal brother.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"At first it did not seem a nice thing to do for me. Soon after I turned 80, my daughter asked to see my drivers license. When I handed it to her she cut it in pieces. She said that so many people my age quit driving the day after they had driven upon a sidewalk or through a crowd on the street and injured or killed people. She decided that I should stop driving the day before that happened to me. She then took me outside and showed me a beautiful red adult tricycle with a huge basket on the back. It was the kind you have to pump, not one with a motor. She gave me red motorcycle helmet, not a skinny bike one, and a city bus pass and the bus schedule. It took a bit of getting over this shock but I understood it was for my good, and anyone else on the street. One neighbor had driven into a garage to have her oil changed and hit the gas instead of brakes and knocked out the back wall of the garage. Another friend my age had driven into and through a store, past the cash registers and doing a lot of damage to the store and ended his car and driving ability. Now I am healthier, stronger and have had many adventures with my trike, and found that the city buses in Santa Barbara are easy to ride and have frequent schedules. For 55 cents a ride I can visit 5 cities in the county. No paying to park, no buying gas and no car insurance. So many people know me now and I even come home as late as 11 pm on the bus without feeling afraid. And, I can ride my trike to the beach and not worry about a place to park.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"Me and my then-girlfriend were in Madeeasy Bhopal, preparing for the GATE exam. We used to be very sincere during that time for our studies. One day, just for fun, I wrote the above letter to her in the class. The reply, I got from her was simply surprising. After reading her reply, I felt as if I always underestimated her sense of humor. The letter roughly translates to - Dear Girlfriend, Greetings, How are you? I am fine here. I hope everything at your place is also fine. I think, I have developed some feelings for you recently. I guess I am in love. Waiting for you to respond, Mayank Tiwari The response from my girlfriend was- Dear Mayank, Greetings, First of all, I would like to thank you that you have developed some feelings for me. May god make you feel like this forever and a day comes when you forget Auntie (my mother). Yours, Girlfriend, waiting to be Tiwari Seeing her response, I was very amused. I rated her answer 9.5/10 and remarked Excellent. A deduction of 0.5 for spelling mistakes and poor handwriting. I took a snap of this picture. The picture quality is not so good because we did not have very good phones at that time. Unfortunately I lost the original letter. I really miss those days. I spent the best time of my life with her in Bhopal. PS: Later I showed this letter to my mother and sister. They also had a good laugh reading her reply.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"My parents have hardly been with each other for 30 months in the past 20 years just for our education. Being born and brought up in a village, it was my parents dream to educate their children and give us the life they could not get. My mother survived in a city without her husband without knowing how to read or write in English language. My father just having passed 10th or 11th grade flew to a foreign country doing the saddest of jobs just so that the high currency exchange can help us survive back here. How he survived on just half of his salary only God knows. There are infinite good things one can do for another but the extraordinary capability of a parent remains unmatched. Cynics might say it\u0027s the Selfish Gene but for me there is nothing else closer to Divinity than my parents.\"\"\", \n    \"\"\"I was working in Pune when I decided to take the entrance test (CAT) for admission to a b-school (IIM). Due to some reasons, I had taken the written test in Calcutta and by the time the results were out I had moved back to Pune and then somewhere near Delhi. This resulted in my interview centres being scattered between Delhi and Calcutta. My father insisted on accompanying me to all the interviews, he was ready to travel from Ranchi to either city. It wasn\u0027t because he was a control freak or that I was a girl; it was  because it was a big thing for me and he wanted to be there for me. After much insistence I managed to dissuade him from traveling all the way to Delhi but he insisted on coming to Calcutta. We arrived at Calcutta separately and he came to pick me at the station; later  he took me to check out the venue a day before the interview. We chatted about various things because I was nervous about the interview. He accompanied me the next day, the venue was a hotel and only candidates were allowed to enter. My father waited the entire day, almost 5-6 hours, in the garden outside, while I went through rounds and rounds of group discussion \u0026 interview. We\u0027re tired by the end of the day and slept off immediately. The next morning I had to catch my train back before my father, I rushed around, fearing that I might miss my train. I was so worried, I didn\u0027t even notice that my father was unusually silent and seemed tired, as if he hadn\u0027t slept properly. I ran across the platform, caught my train, didn\u0027t even say bye to my father whose train was 2 platforms away but he had still come to see me off. Months later, my results were out, I had got a call from several b-schools. A month before my joining one, my father passed away unexpectedly. My mother told me later that he was suffering from TB, that he had been having problems for quite a while, that that was the reason why he looked so tired the day we\u0027re rushing through Calcutta to catch my train. Despite his illness, despite the fact that I could have got calls from the b-schools that interviewed me in Delhi, my father came to be with me on one of my most crucial moments.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"My sister and I had not seen each other in years. We lived in different states, and it got to the point of just birthday and Christmas greetings. My husband had been very ill, and he ended up committing suicide. I never saw it coming. I thought that we would fight to keep us together always. When that happened, my whole world collapsed. I could barely speak. He was the one. The only. The love of my life. My sister and I talked on the phone and she knew I wasn’t “right.” She dropped everything, her whole life, to come and be with me and to take care of all the details that are necessary when someone dies. She stayed with me for a long time, but she had to get back to her life. I started getting professional help. I needed it before all this happened, but my big sister always and forever had been and will be my protector and champion.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"One of many nice things I’ve experienced, probably the most unexpected and welcome was my middle brother (third of six kids), not offering, but insisting that he was coming to take care of me for a few weeks after my first hip joint replacement surgery so that I could avoid a rehab center. Six years older than I, our relationship was distant. We had really only known one another as children, and those were some crazy years. Those few weeks were some of the best ever. He arrived, took me to surgery, came to the hospital to spend all day with me for four days, brought me decent food (the hospital food was inedible) and brought me home, where he doted on me and treated me like a princess, catering to my every whim. He helped me with my physical therapy, and he went grocery shopping in search of my favorite foods as a way to get me to eat. He played with my cats, scooped the litter box, did the laundry and cleaned my house from top to bottom, even though he had a painfully bum knee. During this time, we had an opportunity to talk, to set the record straight about a few things and to just be. We came to know one another as adults and from there, our relationship blossomed. We spoke weekly and I visited him and his family regularly, getting to know and appreciate my sister-in-law, nieces, and nephew. Unfortunately, three years and a month later, he died unexpectedly. I never dreamed I would miss him so much. We both learned many lessons from that two weeks together, but none more important than that who we were as children does not have to determine who we are as adults. As a parting note, I want to acknowledge my oldest brother and his lovely wife, who last year, came and took wonderful care of me during and after my second hip joint replacement. For having had such a dysfunctional childhood, we seem to have turned out quite alright.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"A former colleague and current friend has an excellent answer.  He was an overworked associate at a large law firm when I met him.  (Now he\u0027s an even more overworked partner.) When asked how he\u0027s doing, he would sigh and say \"\"well, y\u0027know... each day\u0027s better than the next.\"\" Most of the time, people react positively to that.  Along the lines of, \"\"great attitude!\"\"  That\u0027s what\u0027s great about this response: it helps you identify people who don\u0027t listen or process well.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"Four days a week, I go to the same coffee shop. You could say I am a regular. The server walks up to me and pulls a chair. “Hi mam, how are you?” I arrange my features into a pleasant smile. “I’m well, Vikas, thank you. How are you doing?” “Good, ma\u0027am. Shall I bring your tea?” “Uh, in a bit. I’d like some water for now.” “Sure, ma\u0027am.” Now, this conversation happens every time. On days when the air smells sweeter than usual and I find myself humming a tune. On days when I am mentally going over the monthly revenue / expenditure while entering. Even on days when I have bawled my eyes out in the car. Ofcourse we’re lying. We are never ‘well’ or ‘good’. We’re always overwhelmed, or confused, or elated, or panicked, or tired, or moody, or in pain. And so is the asker of the rhetoric question. The answer to your question is this. If you are feeling awful, call the person who you can discuss your problem with. Tell them you’re feeling awful and talk about it. To the rest of the World, be prepared to lie . Come on: you’ll get nothing by being honest in this situation. It’s not that nobody cares about your problems (though most people don’t), it’s just that telling them won’t help you at all. The How’re You? conversation is simply a way of acknowledging the existence of another person. It’s like “Hey, you’re here!” The answer to which is, “Yes, and so are you!” That’s about it. So, hitting acquaintances with the loaded phrase ~ ‘ I’m awful’ ~ won’t help you in any scenario. And you don’t want to flummox your barista by putting them in a situation they can do nothing about.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"I hate the question. Most people don’t take a look at the person they’re asking it to. One day I was feeling terrible. I’d not slept the previous night, awake from depression and it clearly showed on my face. My hair was askew, my eyes were red and eyelids were baggy. I looked like I’d been hungover, only worse. I saw a friend of mine who had food problems, and she looked paler than usual. I went up to her and softly said, “You look tired.” and smiled with as much empathy as I could muster. She came up to me later and we talked for a couple of hours about our problems and I’d made a close friend. Most people who have come to terms with illness would love a quiet acknowledgement of the fact that they are going through something difficult. When someone asks you how you feel and you feel unwell, its usually not someone who understands what its like to have really bad days. I usually change the subject without answering the question. A comment about the weather or sports or class or something is usually enough. If its someone who genuinely cares about me and has made a slight, I tell them the truth. I share a common moment with them, and we both go away happier, more fulfilled.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"On most occasions someone asking how you are doing is a courtesy and greeting rather than a desire to get an in-depth recap of your current state of affairs. This is not an opportunity to go into specifics about any illness you may be experiencing or emotional issues you are dealing with. Etiquette dictates that you reply in a short meaningful way that shows appreciation for their interest. For example: \"\"I am a little under the weather to day, but expect to be back to 100% soon. Thank you fro asking. How are you doing?\"\" If \"\"under the weather\"\" is not a common idiom: \"\"Honestly, I am not feeling well, but expect to fully recover soon. I appreciate you asking. How have you been doing? If you are feeling awful emotionally then try:  I appreciate you asking. I am a bit out of sorts today. It has been a trying week, but these things always pass. How have you been doing?\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"Never lie. Not even when someone doesn’t really care what the answer is. So if you’re feeling shitty, say so. If you feel fine, say so but only if it’s true. This should jolt the person out of the non-caring attitude at least somewhat, to an “OH! I’m sorry… I hope you feel better soon. Have a better day,” and then they leave, flustered and not feeling right over what else they should say. But you - you are a feeling person and you will react in a truthful manner, at least I hope you will. If you know the person, “Is there anything I can do to help?” “Do you need a ride to the doctor’s office?” or something like that. Don’t just say I’ll pray for you - throwing words around at an invisible, unseen, unproven god will do no good, but actual help will. So answer the question honestly and be helpful with those who answer you honestly. It will make a better world.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"when someone asks how you are feeling and you are feeling down, it\u0027s okay to be honest and express how you feel. You could say something like, \"\"I\u0027m feeling a bit down at the moment, but thank you for asking.\"\" If you feel comfortable, you could also elaborate on why you\u0027re feeling down or share any thoughts or feelings you\u0027re experiencing. It\u0027s important to remember that it\u0027s okay to not feel okay sometimes and that talking to someone about how you\u0027re feeling can help you feel better. i hope this can help you thanks\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"I get that a lot and I don\u0027t want to burden people with my symptoms of depression and anxiety because that really turns them off. But then I ask myself are they asking because they genuinely want to know and be of help or just to pry or because it\u0027s socially the thing you do when you start a conversation with someone.  I think it depends on the person and what your relation is with them. The answer may vary with different people.  If you trust the person and know they\u0027re coming from a good place then tell them how you really feel. If you think the person doesn\u0027t really care I would say keep it short and simple. You can just say I\u0027m not up for discussing it right now and leave it at that.\"\"\"  , \n    \"\"\"What a question. Usually when it is someone who you meet for the first time or in passing, it is asked offhandedly. In such a case, I say “doing well” or “hi, nice to meet you also.” Only when it is someone you know or someone who is being sincere should you open up to them, or tell them what is really on your mind. Generally, it is a simple and easy question to ask which means you should give a simple answer to keep the conversation moving.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"I express my gratitude that i am being given the opportunity to overcome a life challenge and while I havent figured out the way past it yet , I am loving the new way Im having to view something. Sometimes its not that your feeling awful. You are most likely in that unknown grey area that accompanies growth and change . we humans like to wallow inthat shit.. I myself love the headlong tackle ..fix your attitude, fix your life. There is no reason that anyone should ever feel awful. Life is about feeling good. so find what feels good and go with it all the way . Its why you incarnated here to create the life you want and if you go around feeling awful…you are going to create for yourself 100% an awful life..its up to you really. So in a roundabout way you could say to the person…” oh actually your query reminded me that I am not feeling as good as I ought……Thanks for bringing that to my attention… and I will presently respond to your question with : wow Im feeling awesome good to be alive…namaste\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"I absolutely hate this question.I generally just tell the truth in my normal life. People strongly dislike me doing this but I don\u0027t care. If you don\u0027t want to know the answer to a question, do not ask it . My personality is such that I can barely tolerate niceties. If a sentence doesn\u0027t have a function, I\u0027m probably not the person to say it to. At the place where I work though I commonly encounter upwards of 300 different people in a day, all of whom I see frequently throughout the month. Many of these people say this to me and I hate it because it is I absolutely hate this question. I\u0027ve written about it a few times on Quora. I generally just tell the truth in my normal life. People strongly dislike me doing this but I don\u0027t care. If you don\u0027t want to know the answer to a question, do not ask it . My personality is such that I can barely tolerate niceties. If a sentence doesn\u0027t have a function, I\u0027m probably not the person to say it to. At the place where I work though I commonly encounter upwards of 300 different people in a day, all of whom I see frequently throughout the month. Many of these people say this to me and I hate it because it is not appropriate to be honest. I am a very honest person and abhor lying, so I very much resent being forced into lying. What I do is answer something like \"\"I\u0027m ok\"\" because it isn\u0027t not true . I am ok because it could be worse. I still resent having to rationalize the truth though. One point of differentiation here however is good friends. They know (or learn quickly) that they should only ask if they actually care. Fake people are not my friends. A close friend who asks this question will get the unfiltered truth.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"I had something very similar happen, and I did tell the company. In high school, I was paid by the hour, and my hours were tracked by how long I was logged in to the computer. The company\u0027s accounting was completely (and poorly) automated. The computer automatically printed the check every two weeks, and logged it in a database. One Friday, I opened my check to discover it was made out for nearly $9,000. I was floored! I was making $10.50 an hour, working 15 hours per week after school. After taxes, I would’ve had to work nearly 15 months to make that. As much as I could use the money, I couldn’t take it. Best case scenario is I scammed the company out of $9 grand, and worst case I’d get caught. Not exactly a win-win. So I kept the check and sent off an email to the CFO. He immediately responded asking me to come in the office the next day. We figured out what happened. The week before, I had had some trouble with my computer, and IT brought a new one. While setting up, he accidentally shifted the time on the calendar forward a month. As far as the software knew, I was working 24 hours a day, every day, for 30 days straight. It gave me A LOT of overtime. The CFO voided the check and issued a new one, and I was given the entire next week off with pay for my honesty (worth about $150 to me). A far cry from $9,000, but I felt good about it.\"\"\" ,\n    \"\"\" When I was in the Navy back in the 70s I was overpaid for a couple of months. I noticed when it started (my paycheck never varied unless I changed withholding or something), so I went to the ship\u0027s disbursing office and told them. The clerk at the counter pulled my file, looked at it, and said, “No, it\u0027s right. We changed something.” I forget what he said the change was - 70s, remember. We were on WESPAC and out in the Indian Ocean for about a month. When I got my check while we were at sea it was for something like $5! Off to disbursing again, got the same clerk, and he told me, “We overpaid you and we\u0027re taking it back. I figured you\u0027d want the money when we were in the PI, so I didn\u0027t pull it back until we were at sea. Your next check will be the same then back to normal.” I lost it, had him bring his Chief to the window, and explained the situation, loudly. He agreed with me, said it should have been fixed immediately when I reported it, let me know the clerk was going to be talked to, but couldn\u0027t do anything about the claw back. That\u0027s how I ended up trying to enjoy myself for 4 days in Singapore with about $20 in my pocket.\"\"\", \n    \"\"\"I was a store manager with a growing chain of convenience stores. I was shot point blank in my right hand with a .44 magnum hollow point bullet, while disarming a suicidal, intoxicated man. It happened on a Saturday evening, in my pasture, of all places. I lost part of my hand, and my ring finger. I returned to work at 6 am Monday morning and worked for 5 weeks. One day, the division manager (who did not appreciate my sense of humor, as I have Wisenheimer disease) came to my store. He said I needed to go on short term disability, and assured me my store would be waiting for me. Two days later, he gave my store to the interim manager. After 5 months, 3 surgeries, and a lot of physical therapy, I returned to work. Owing to the fact that I am righthanded, and the substantial nerve damage in my hand and wrist, I was unsure of my ability to fulfill manager’s role (lots of physical, as well mental work.) I decided to step down to assistant instead of asking for another store. My pay was set at the position\u0027s ceiling. When I received my paycheck, it was more than I was entitled to. Payroll had taken my manager salary, and divided it by my hours. I called my area manager, and he contacted them, and had it corrected. I eventually stepped back up to store manager, and was quite successful. Ironically, the company restructured, and the division manager went to work at the bank. Every time I did any banking, and he was working the drive-thru, he would make rude remarks, call me Lefty, and point out my hand to any other tellers there. One day, I sent my assistant to pick up my change order. Old dm had prepared it. When I counted it, there was an extra $500. I immediately called the bank, and sent my assistant back with the “free\"\" money. After that, old dm would suck up to me and call me his “best friend\"\", because he had had no idea to whom he had given the stray money. It was more annoying than the previous rudeness.\"\"\", \n    \"\"\"While going back to college as a mature student, I kept my part-time job in a small bank as a teller. My 2 children and I were living on a stipend that was part of a scholarship I won, plus the wages from this job. The bank also provided benefits for my little family that I really appreciated. Anyway, in June I got a pay cheque that was about $2K more than I expected. The money would have been very welcome, but I knew I couldn’t keep it. I went to my manager to report the overpayment. He said that June was when the bank paid out vacation pay and it was based on the formula of 4% of your previous year’s income. The previous year I had worked full time before I started college, so that was why the payment was so generous. My boss told me to spend it in good conscience! My kids got some extras that month and I put a chunk away to buy them full winter outerwear come autumn.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"I once had a contract job for an insurance company for 1 year that resulted in them paying me more than they should have. My leave entitlement for that year was 14 days. As i have worked for 11 months, i was entitled to 14 days holiday that year. However when my employment ended i had already taken 19 days, which is 5 days more than I was entitled to as a result of an ailment. When I got paid eventually, I realized that my absent days were not deducted from my paycheck. Initially, I thought it was a mistake on their path and I felt reluctant to call their attention to it since I wouldn’t have to deal with them again but I couldn’t live with myself if i had gone through with it. I reported the situation to the company and to my surprise they said it was a conscious decision on their path to commend me for a job well done. I felt all shades of good that day, because it was a win-win situation for me, I secured my money and most importantly my reputation Oh! by the way, I think extra two zeros to your weekly paycheck will not be a secret for long even if you tried to keep it as one.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"Absolutely I would. I have done before. Back in the day (late 90s) the company I was working for decided to incentivise the UMTS bid and pre-sales team based on how the bids performed in relation to expectations with that customer. For instance the target may have been to make the shortlist of bids, if we made it on to the shortlist we got a £5000 bonus. If we exceeded the expectations, i.e. made the final three instead of just making the shortlist, we got £10,000. One payday I opened up my banking app and discovered £10k extra in my account above what I’d been expecting. I then checked my payslip to see I’d been paid a £10k bonus that shouldn’t have been there. I went straight to HR and fessed up. I wasn’t the only one who’d benefitted from an inputting error, and the money would have been clawed back at the next payroll run. I decided to pre-empt it and wrote a cheque for the difference that I’d been overpaid after tax and NI. I also received a thank you letter from the the HR director for my honesty. Part of the reason I confessed however was because the overpayment would have put me into a higher tax bracket for that financial year costing me more next year.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"I once worked for a very large IT company. Due to a quirky year, I was going to get a big bonus. Over $100K. Right as the bonuses were being awarded I transferred to another division. They were run as truly separate companies and I would get a W-2 from both that year. Then they both paid me the bonus. Oh it was tempting but I wasn\u0027t going to jail for that. I called the payroll department and told them. There was full silence for a solid minute. He came back on and asked if I realized they were separate companies with separate payroll departments. I assured him I did. His quote: “Why did you tell us? We never would have figured it out.” I gave him the expected answer about it being wrong. Sure but I liked my freedom too.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"In my case I had ended my time as a temporary employee and was now working as a consultant. I did not have an error on my paycheck as such; rather, I kept getting paychecks even though I was no longer an employee. I immediately called the pay office and reported it but amazingly they were unsure of how to deal with it. Apparently there was no mechanism that would allow me to simply return the physical check. My concern was that the paycheck amount would somehow show up as earnings on my tax form and I expressed this concern in an e-mail to the pay office. No response but two weeks later I got another check. Repeat previous action and once again express concern about taxes. This went on for weeks and I ended up with over $10,000 in checks that weren’t mine. Eventually I was asked to deposit all the checks and send them a person check in the amount that I had received. They undertook to ensure that the amount that would show up on my tax form was correct. Fat chance of that. I got a call from the pay office on the day that they were required to submit the tax data telling me that alas my records had not been corrected and the tax form would show the checks as taxable earnings. My response was to inform them that they had a legal obligation to get the forms in on time and correct and that I had been warning them of the problem for three months. I informed them that I would issue formal complaints about their failure. Finally I told the clerk that I expected the correct T4 form even if her manager had to fill it out by hand using a quill pen and her own blood as ink (I was getting pretty upset at that point). I got a call at 11:00 pm telling me that the correct number had been inserted into the system Did I mention that I worked for the Government of Canada. Most Canadians will know that the implementation of a new civil service pay system has been a billion dollar debacle and won’t be surprised but this pre-dated the new system. This was the old system which actually worked if the staff knew what they were doing.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"Many years ago my grandfather faced a similar situation except for 2 key points: the amount of money and the payment method. The amount of money in excess was no more than 5 or 10% of the monthly salary that was low at that time, so any extra income was really much more needed than now. He was paid the monthly salary in cash inside a sealed envelope. No computers, internet or email what means that the error could be detected but impossible to identify who received the extra money among all the workers. He resolved to return the money as he was a honest person. When I asked him whether he had the temptation to keep the money he said: “no, but keeping the money would have been a bad idea. I had not been working there for more than a few months only, so the manager could have added extra money to my envelope to get to know whether I could be trusted or not”. So If you want to keep the money you would have to wait until the second time that mistake occurs.\"\"\" , \n    \"\"\"I would let them know, because where I live, it’s illegal to keep cash or stuff if you find it or are given it in error and can ID the owner or who has made the error. As an example, if you find a wallet with $100 cash and it contains the ID of the wallet owner, you can not lawfully take ownership, just because it’s in your possession. It’s obvious to the legal system, who the true owner of the wallet is. If you keep the cash and later get caught, it’ll will be referred to as “thief by finding” in the arresting documents. It’s the same with a bank account. If your account was accidently credited with cash, you know is not yours, you are obligated to let the bank know about the mistake. You can’t keep it and playing dumb won’t get you off the hook for the repayment. The bank will go after you if it thinks you were trying to keep the cash instead of repaying it. And that will probably mean a “thief by finding” charge.\"\"\"\n    \n]\nnum_answers \u003d len(example_answers)\nnum_questions \u003d len(questions_list)\nanswers_per_question \u003d 10\nif num_answers !\u003d num_questions * answers_per_question:\n    print(f\"경고: 제공된 답변 수({num_answers})가 예상된 답변 수({num_questions * answers_per_question})와 일치하지 않습니다.\")\n    # 여기서 실행을 중단하거나, 데이터 구조를 조정해야 할 수 있습니다.\n    # 우선 진행하되, 결과 해석에 주의가 필요합니다.\n\nhuman_data \u003d {\n    \u0027model\u0027: [\u0027human\u0027] * num_answers,\n    \u0027Question\u0027: [q for q in questions_list for _ in range(answers_per_question)],\n    \u0027Attempt\u0027: list(range(1, answers_per_question + 1)) * num_questions,\n    \u0027Answer\u0027: example_answers\n}\ndata_human \u003d pd.DataFrame(human_data)\ndata_human\n\tmodel\tQuestion\tAttempt\tAnswer\n0\thuman\tHave you ever made a mistake that ended up sav...\t1\tWhen I was in my early twenties, my friends an...\n1\thuman\tHave you ever made a mistake that ended up sav...\t2\tYeah, for some reason I terribly overslept and...\n2\thuman\tHave you ever made a mistake that ended up sav...\t3\tYes. It was November 2018, I was going back ho...\n3\thuman\tHave you ever made a mistake that ended up sav...\t4\tYes, becau